  "result": "Not quite. A language model doesn't remember anything on its own. Each call starts blank, and it only sees the text you send in that call.\n\nHere's what that looks like. Last week, Sam tells your assistant, \"I finished the laser cutter training.\" This week Sam asks, \"Can I book the laser cutter?\" If your program sends only that one question, the model has no idea who Sam is or what he said. It will guess, or ask him again.\n\nMemory works when your program does it:\n1. After each session, your code saves the useful facts, for example `{\"sam\": {\"laser_cutter_trained\": true}}`.\n2. At the start of the next session, your code looks Sam up and puts that fact into the text it sends the model.\n3. The model then \"remembers\", but only because you handed it the note.\n\nEven within one chat, the app re-sends the earlier messages every time. That's how it feels like memory.\n\nThis matters for your design review, because \"what do we save between sessions, and what do we re-send?\" is your decision. It also affects cost, since everything you re-send is text you pay for.\n\nDoes that make sense? If you were saving just one fact per student for next week, which one would you pick?",
